\par 2. It will be necessary that we find the names of the months ourselves.\emdash  I myself will begin:\par 3. The first month of the year is called January. Indeed it opens the door of the [incoming] (}{\i\f211 beginning}{\f208 ) year.\par 4.\emdash  [It's] not from door, but from the god Janus [that] it takes (}{\i\f211 leads}{\f208 ) its name.\par 5.\emdash  There follow February, March, April, May, June, Quintile, Sextile\u8230\'c9\par 6.\emdash  You are saying old stuff! Do you ignore [that] the last two months took (}{\i\f211 to have led}{\f208 ) their names from our princes Julius Caesar and Augustus?\par 7. And that's why we are now meant to say July for Quintile and Augustus for Sextile.\par 8. The premiers of all nations change names more easily than customs.\par 9. The following months were not changed however: September, October, Novemeber, December.\par 10.\emdash  When does spring begin?\par 11.\emdash  When the end of the month of March approaches and when the trees bring forth buds.\par 12. Summer (aestas) takes (}{\i\f211 leads}{\f208 ) its name from "aestus", which means "severe heat".\par 13. In the autumn leaves fall, even tax [bills] (}{\i\f211 leaves [of paper]}{\f208 ) (which announce the tax to be paid).\par 14. In winter snow covers the mountains; the lakes are frozen with ice; it is the time of winter sports.\par \par NOTES\par }{\b\f210 1. M}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 nsis}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 , m., parisyllabic, so the genitive plural is in -}{\b\f210 ium}{\f208 .\par }{\b\f210 2. J}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 nus}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 i}{\f208 , m., }{\i\f211 Janus}{\f208 , a very old Roman god, who was represented as having a secound face at the nape of the neck, which must have given him a remarkable field of vision! The words for the months are }{\i\f211 adjectives}{\f208 . Those which belong to the 3}{\f208\super rd}{\f208  declension (}{\b\f210 Apr}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 lis}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 Oct}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ber}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 bris}{\f208 , etc.) from therefore their ablative in -}{\b\f210 i}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 Apr}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 li}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 Oct}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 bri}{\f208 ).\par }{\b\f210 3. Pr}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 nceps}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 ipis}{\f208 , is an adjective used here as a noun: }{\i\f211 the first}{\f208  (}{\i\f211 or premier of the State}{\f208 ).\par }{\b\f210 4. Pro}{\f208  + abl., preposition, }{\i\f211 instead of}{\f208  \u8230\'c9\par }{\b\f210 5. Fac}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 lius}{\f208 , adv., }{\i\f211 more easily}{\f208 , comparative of }{\b\f210 f}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 cile}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 easily}{\f208  (cf. L 12); the corresponding adjective is }{\b\f210 f}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 cilis}{\f208 , n. }{\b\f210 f}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 cile}{\f208 ; comparative }{\b\f210 fac}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 lior}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 ius}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 easier}{\f208 ; superlative (irregular) }{\b\f210 fac}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 llimus}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 a}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 um}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 easiest}{\f208 . You can see that the nominative-accusative neuter of the adjective and comparative can be transformed into an adverb. The superlative of the adverb is obtained by replacing with an }{\b\f210 e}{\f208  the ending of the superlative of the adjective: }{\b\f210 fac}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 llime}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 most easily}{\f208 .\par }{\b\f210 6. Sept}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 mber}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 Oct}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 ber}{\f208 , etc., come from }{\b\f210 s}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 ptem}{\f208 , o}{\b\f210 cto}{\f208 , etc., because there was a time when the year began on the 1}{\f208\super st}{\f208  of March, which similarly explains the old }{\b\f210 Quint}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 lis}{\f208  and }{\b\f210 Sext}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 lis}{\f208  (}{\b\f210 qu}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 ntus}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 s}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 xtus}{\f208 ).\par }{\b\f210 7. Ver}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 v}{\f208 e}{\b\f210 ris}{\f208 , n., }{\i\f211 the spring}{\f208  (Sp. }{\i\f211 la primavera}{\f208 ); ae}{\b\f210 stas}{\f208 , -a}{\b\f210 tis}{\f208 , f., }{\i\f211 the summer}{\f208  (Fr. }{\i\f211 l'\u233\'8et\u233\'8e}{\f208 ); }{\b\f210 aut}{\f208 u}{\b\f210 mnus}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 i}{\f208 , m., }{\i\f211 the autumn}{\f208  (Fr. }{\i\f211 l'automne}{\f208 ); }{\b\f210 h}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 ems}{\f208 , }{\b\f210 h}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 emis}{\f208 , f., }{\i\f211 the winter}{\f208  (Fr. }{\i\f211 l'hiver}{\f208 ). Note that the genders of most of these nouns differ from those of their Romance counterparts.\par }{\b\f210 8. }{\f208 Ae}{\b\f210 stus}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 us}{\f208 , m., }{\i\f211 sultriness}{\f208  (cf. }{\b\f210 aestu}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 re}{\f208 , L 32, N 2). Note that }{\b\f210 c}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 lor}{\f208 , -o}{\b\f210 ris}{\f208 , is masculine, as }{\i\f211 calore}{\f208  is in Italian.\par }{\b\f210 9. F}{\f208 o}{\b\f210 lium}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 ii}{\f208 , n., }{\i\f211 the leaf}{\f208 , }{\i\f211 sheet}{\f208 , in every sense of these words.\par }{\b\f210 10. Vect}{\f208 i}{\b\f210 gal}{\f208 , -a}{\b\f210 lis}{\f208 , n., }{\i\f211 the tax}{\f208 , is a false }{\i\f211 imparisyllabic}{\f208  (contraction of }{\b\f210 vectig}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 le}{\f208 , -}{\b\f210 is}{\f208 ) so the singular ablative is in -}{\b\f210 i}{\f208 , the nominative-accusative plural in -}{\b\f210 ia}{\f208  and the genitive plural in -}{\b\f210 ium}{\f208 , like the parisyllabics. However, in phrase 13, }{\b\f210 vectig}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 lia}{\f208  does not come from the noun, but from the corresponding adjective: }{\b\f210 vectig}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 lis}{\f208  (n. }{\b\f210 vectig}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 le}{\f208 ), }{\i\f211 relating to tax}{\f208 , which has otherwise many forms in common with the noun.\par \par And to finish, let's see more closely the 4}{\f208\super th}{\f208  declension, which includes:\par \par 1}{\f208\super st}{\f208  On the one hand, words in -}{\b\f210 us}{\f208 , mostly }{\i\f211 masculines}{\f208 ; but }{\b\f210 m}{\f208 a}{\b\f210 n